#. type: Yaml Front Matter Hash Value: description
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Design principles of operator representations for the latest version of "
"Qiskit bosons"
msgstr ""

#. type: Yaml Front Matter Hash Value: title
#, no-wrap
msgid "Design principles of operator representations"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "<span id=\"operators-explanation\" />\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title #
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Design principles of operator representations {#design-principles-of-"
"operator-representations}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"This guide explains how a "
"[`BosonOperator`](https://docs.quantum.ibm.com/api/qiskit-bosons/operators-"
"boson-operator#qiskit_bosons.operators.BosonOperator "
"\"qiskit_bosons.operators.BosonOperator\") is organized and how to work with"
" it: how to build one, how to inspect and combine operators, and how to "
"bring two operators into a form where they can be compared. Start here when "
"you write down a Hamiltonian.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"A [`BosonOperator`](https://docs.quantum.ibm.com/api/qiskit-"
"bosons/operators-boson-operator#qiskit_bosons.operators.BosonOperator "
"\"qiskit_bosons.operators.BosonOperator\") is an exact sum of products of "
"bosonic creation and annihilation operators with complex coefficients. It "
"carries no occupation cutoff. A bosonic mode holds arbitrarily many bosons, "
"so its Fock space is infinite-dimensional, and a truncation enters only when"
" the operator is mapped onto qubits (see [Truncate the Fock "
"space](mappers#truncation-explanation)). Everything in this guide therefore "
"happens in the exact algebra of the canonical commutation relations,\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"$$\n"
"[b_i, b_j^\\dagger] = \\delta_{ij}, \\qquad [b_i, b_j] = [b_i^\\dagger, b_j^\\dagger] = 0 \\, .\n"
"$$\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Two consequences shape the interface. A mode index can repeat within a "
"single term, because $b_i^\\dagger b_i^\\dagger$ is a valid operator that "
"creates two bosons. Also, reordering two factors never introduces a sign, "
"although moving an annihilation operator past a creation operator on the "
"same mode adds a term, $b_i b_i^\\dagger = b_i^\\dagger b_i + 1$.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Storage {#storage}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"An operator keeps its terms in four flat arrays, similar to the compressed "
"sparse row format of a sparse matrix:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"|              |                                                                                         |\n"
"| ------------ | --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n"
"| `coeffs`     | One complex coefficient per term.                                                       |\n"
"| `actions`    | One boolean per factor: `True` for a creation and `False` for an annihilation operator. |\n"
"| `modes`      | One mode index per factor.                                                              |\n"
"| `boundaries` | The positions in `actions` and `modes` where each term starts and ends.                 |\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The constructor takes these four arrays directly. The operator below has "
"three terms, namely $2 b_0^\\dagger b_1$, $-i b_1^\\dagger b_1^\\dagger$, "
"and the constant $0.5$, and you can read the arrays back with the `get_*` "
"methods:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The first term spans positions 0 to 2 of `actions` and `modes`, the second "
"spans 2 to 4, and the third spans 4 to 4, which is how the constant term, a "
"term without factors, is stored. The repeated mode `1` in the second term is"
" preserved as written; a term is never dropped or merged because a mode "
"index appears twice.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"<Admonition title=\"Note\" type=\"note\">\n"
"  An operator built with [`from_dict()`](https://docs.quantum.ibm.com/api/qiskit-bosons/operators-boson-operator#qiskit_bosons.operators.BosonOperator.from_dict \"qiskit_bosons.operators.BosonOperator.from_dict\") does not store its terms in the order of the dictionary, so its arrays can list the terms in a different order from run to run. The operator itself is the same, and nothing below that compares or prints operators depends on the stored order.\n"
"</Admonition>\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The layout makes construction and arithmetic cheap, with one consequence: an"
" operator can hold the same term several times at once. Sums and products "
"append terms and do not merge them, so call "
"[`simplify()`](https://docs.quantum.ibm.com/api/qiskit-bosons/operators-"
"operator-trait#qiskit_bosons.operators.OperatorTrait.simplify "
"\"qiskit_bosons.operators.OperatorTrait.simplify\") when you need unique "
"terms (see [Do arithmetic](#operators-arithmetic)).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Build operators {#build-operators}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The most direct way to build an operator is "
"[`from_dict()`](https://docs.quantum.ibm.com/api/qiskit-bosons/operators-"
"boson-operator#qiskit_bosons.operators.BosonOperator.from_dict "
"\"qiskit_bosons.operators.BosonOperator.from_dict\"), which takes a "
"dictionary from a tuple of factors to a coefficient. The functions "
"[`cre()`](https://docs.quantum.ibm.com/api/qiskit-bosons/operators-"
"cre#qiskit_bosons.operators.cre \"qiskit_bosons.operators.cre\") and "
"[`ann()`](https://docs.quantum.ibm.com/api/qiskit-bosons/operators-"
"ann#qiskit_bosons.operators.ann \"qiskit_bosons.operators.ann\") create the "
"factors, and the empty tuple is the constant term:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"In the printed form, `+0` stands for $b_0^\\dagger$ and `-0` for $b_0$. The "
"constructors [`zero()`](https://docs.quantum.ibm.com/api/qiskit-"
"bosons/operators-operator-trait#qiskit_bosons.operators.OperatorTrait.zero "
"\"qiskit_bosons.operators.OperatorTrait.zero\") and "
"[`one()`](https://docs.quantum.ibm.com/api/qiskit-bosons/operators-operator-"
"trait#qiskit_bosons.operators.OperatorTrait.one "
"\"qiskit_bosons.operators.OperatorTrait.one\") return the additive and the "
"multiplicative identity, and "
"[`from_terms()`](https://docs.quantum.ibm.com/api/qiskit-bosons/operators-"
"boson-operator#qiskit_bosons.operators.BosonOperator.from_terms "
"\"qiskit_bosons.operators.BosonOperator.from_terms\") rebuilds an operator "
"from the iterator of "
"[`iter_terms()`](https://docs.quantum.ibm.com/api/qiskit-bosons/operators-"
"boson-operator#qiskit_bosons.operators.BosonOperator.iter_terms "
"\"qiskit_bosons.operators.BosonOperator.iter_terms\").\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The operator library provides the dimensionless position and momentum "
"quadratures,\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"$$\n"
"\\hat{q} = \\frac{b^\\dagger + b}{\\sqrt{2}}, \\qquad\n"
"\\hat{p} = \\frac{i \\left(b^\\dagger - b\\right)}{\\sqrt{2}} \\, ,\n"
"$$\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"as [`position()`](https://docs.quantum.ibm.com/api/qiskit-bosons/operators-"
"library-position#qiskit_bosons.operators.library.position "
"\"qiskit_bosons.operators.library.position\") and "
"[`momentum()`](https://docs.quantum.ibm.com/api/qiskit-bosons/operators-"
"library-momentum#qiskit_bosons.operators.library.momentum "
"\"qiskit_bosons.operators.library.momentum\"). Together with the commutator "
"functions of the same module, you can check the algebra directly:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The module also provides "
"[`anti_commutator()`](https://docs.quantum.ibm.com/api/qiskit-"
"bosons/operators-library-anti-"
"commutator#qiskit_bosons.operators.library.anti_commutator "
"\"qiskit_bosons.operators.library.anti_commutator\") and "
"[`double_commutator()`](https://docs.quantum.ibm.com/api/qiskit-"
"bosons/operators-library-double-"
"commutator#qiskit_bosons.operators.library.double_commutator "
"\"qiskit_bosons.operators.library.double_commutator\"). All three take "
"operators of the same type and return a new operator.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "<span id=\"operators-product-order\" />\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Mind the order of a product {#mind-the-order-of-a-product}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Two operators can be multiplied in two ways, and they are easy to confuse. "
"The `@` operator is the mathematical product, so `a @ b` is $AB$. The `&` "
"operator is *composition*, which follows Qiskit’s convention of reading from"
" left to right: `a & b` first applies `a` and then `b`, so it is $BA$. The "
"commutator functions use `@`, so `commutator(a, b)` is $[A, B]$. Building "
"the bracket yourself out of `&` silently negates it:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Inspect an operator {#inspect-an-operator}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"An operator is not directly iterable, because its terms live in the flat "
"arrays above. Use [`iter_terms()`](https://docs.quantum.ibm.com/api/qiskit-"
"bosons/operators-operator-"
"trait#qiskit_bosons.operators.OperatorTrait.iter_terms "
"\"qiskit_bosons.operators.OperatorTrait.iter_terms\") instead, which yields "
"a list of `(is_creation, mode)` pairs and a coefficient for each term. The "
"iterator returns copies, so changing them does not change the operator.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Several methods summarize the structure of an operator:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"[`get_support()`](https://docs.quantum.ibm.com/api/qiskit-bosons/operators-"
"operator-trait#qiskit_bosons.operators.OperatorTrait.get_support "
"\"qiskit_bosons.operators.OperatorTrait.get_support\") is the set of modes "
"the operator acts on, "
"[`min_num_modes()`](https://docs.quantum.ibm.com/api/qiskit-"
"bosons/operators-boson-"
"operator#qiskit_bosons.operators.BosonOperator.min_num_modes "
"\"qiskit_bosons.operators.BosonOperator.min_num_modes\") is the smallest "
"register that can hold it, and "
"[`max_rank()`](https://docs.quantum.ibm.com/api/qiskit-bosons/operators-"
"boson-operator#qiskit_bosons.operators.BosonOperator.max_rank "
"\"qiskit_bosons.operators.BosonOperator.max_rank\") is the length of its "
"longest term.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"<Admonition title=\"Note\" type=\"note\">\n"
"  A **mode** is an abstract index that labels one bosonic degree of freedom. It has no meaning of its own: you determine which site of a lattice, which vibrational coordinate or which optical port a mode stands for. The [`BosonicCircuit`](https://docs.quantum.ibm.com/api/qiskit-bosons/circuit-bosonic-circuit#qiskit_bosons.circuit.BosonicCircuit \"qiskit_bosons.circuit.BosonicCircuit\") uses the same indices (see [Work with bosonic circuits](circuit#bosonic-circuit-explanation)), so an operator and the gates that carry it agree on what mode `2` is without either one knowing what it represents. Nothing in the operator fixes how many levels a mode has, which is also why the register size is only a lower bound; [`min_num_modes()`](https://docs.quantum.ibm.com/api/qiskit-bosons/operators-boson-operator#qiskit_bosons.operators.BosonOperator.min_num_modes \"qiskit_bosons.operators.BosonOperator.min_num_modes\") is the smallest register that holds the operator, not a property of it.\n"
"</Admonition>\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The occupation methods report how many bosons a single term can place in one"
" mode. This is a lower bound on the cutoff that a mapping needs. It is not a"
" sufficient cutoff, because repeated applications of the operator, as in a "
"time evolution, can populate higher levels (see [Truncate the Fock "
"space](mappers#truncation-explanation)):\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Two further checks look at conserved quantities. An operator conserves the "
"particle number when every term has as many creation as annihilation "
"operators. Passing a list of block sizes tests the same condition within "
"each block of consecutive modes:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Finally, [`relabel_modes()`](https://docs.quantum.ibm.com/api/qiskit-"
"bosons/operators-operator-"
"trait#qiskit_bosons.operators.OperatorTrait.relabel_modes "
"\"qiskit_bosons.operators.OperatorTrait.relabel_modes\") renames modes: mode"
" `i` becomes `permutation[i]`. The list must be long enough to index every "
"mode of the operator and must not repeat an entry:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "<span id=\"operators-arithmetic\" />\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Do arithmetic {#do-arithmetic}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Operators support addition, subtraction, negation, and multiplication and "
"division by a number, in the usual operator form or in place:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The three terms of `total` were never merged, so the operator held `b_dag` "
"twice until [`simplify()`](https://docs.quantum.ibm.com/api/qiskit-"
"bosons/operators-operator-"
"trait#qiskit_bosons.operators.OperatorTrait.simplify "
"\"qiskit_bosons.operators.OperatorTrait.simplify\") combined them. The "
"method sums the coefficients of identical terms and drops those whose total "
"magnitude is below a tolerance. It is a structural operation: it compares "
"the written factors and does *not* apply the commutation relations. The "
"related method [`ichop()`](https://docs.quantum.ibm.com/api/qiskit-"
"bosons/operators-operator-trait#qiskit_bosons.operators.OperatorTrait.ichop "
"\"qiskit_bosons.operators.OperatorTrait.ichop\") removes small coefficients "
"in place without merging first, so call "
"[`simplify()`](https://docs.quantum.ibm.com/api/qiskit-bosons/operators-"
"operator-trait#qiskit_bosons.operators.OperatorTrait.simplify "
"\"qiskit_bosons.operators.OperatorTrait.simplify\") before "
"[`ichop()`](https://docs.quantum.ibm.com/api/qiskit-bosons/operators-"
"operator-trait#qiskit_bosons.operators.OperatorTrait.ichop "
"\"qiskit_bosons.operators.OperatorTrait.ichop\") when an operator can hold "
"duplicate terms.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Products use `@` or `&` as described above, and `**` raises an operator to "
"an integer power:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The result lists the four orderings as written. Reducing them to a single "
"form needs the commutation relations, which the next section applies.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The adjoint reverses the order of the factors in each term, exchanges "
"creation and annihilation operators, and conjugates the coefficients. The "
"method [`is_hermitian()`](https://docs.quantum.ibm.com/api/qiskit-"
"bosons/operators-operator-"
"trait#qiskit_bosons.operators.OperatorTrait.is_hermitian "
"\"qiskit_bosons.operators.OperatorTrait.is_hermitian\") tests whether an "
"operator equals its adjoint:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"A lone hopping term is not Hermitian, which matters when you group terms for"
" a time evolution: each group must be Hermitian on its own (see [Group "
"operator terms: use the operator structure](grouping#grouping-"
"explanation)).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Normal order and compare operators {#normal-order-and-compare-operators}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Two products of ladder operators can be the same operator and still look "
"different, because $b\\,b^\\dagger$ and $b^\\dagger b + 1$ are equal in the "
"algebra. Operators that are equal in this sense need not be stored equally, "
"so comparing them needs the commutation relations. The method "
"[`normal_ordered()`](https://docs.quantum.ibm.com/api/qiskit-"
"bosons/operators-boson-"
"operator#qiskit_bosons.operators.BosonOperator.normal_ordered "
"\"qiskit_bosons.operators.BosonOperator.normal_ordered\") applies them: it "
"moves every creation operator to the left of every annihilation operator and"
" adds a term whenever a pair on the same mode is exchanged.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The ordered operator is returned as an unmerged sum, with five stored terms "
"that reduce to three after "
"[`simplify()`](https://docs.quantum.ibm.com/api/qiskit-bosons/operators-"
"operator-trait#qiskit_bosons.operators.OperatorTrait.simplify "
"\"qiskit_bosons.operators.OperatorTrait.simplify\") merges them. Normal "
"ordering never makes a term vanish: $b^\\dagger b^\\dagger$ and every other "
"repeated factor stay.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The `sandwich` argument sets how the modes inside the two groups of factors "
"are arranged. By default both groups are sorted in descending order. With "
"`sandwich=True`, the creation operators are ascending and the annihilation "
"operators descending, which puts the larger indices towards the middle:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The `==` operator tests exact equality of the stored terms, with no "
"tolerance and no simplification, so it can report a difference between two "
"operators that are mathematically equal. The method "
"[`equiv()`](https://docs.quantum.ibm.com/api/qiskit-bosons/operators-boson-"
"operator#qiskit_bosons.operators.BosonOperator.equiv "
"\"qiskit_bosons.operators.BosonOperator.equiv\") brings the difference of "
"the two operators to normal order first and then tests that every "
"coefficient is below a tolerance. It is the comparison you almost always "
"want:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The method `simplify` alone leaves the two forms apart, because it merges "
"only identical written terms. Likewise, "
"[`is_hermitian()`](https://docs.quantum.ibm.com/api/qiskit-bosons/operators-"
"operator-trait#qiskit_bosons.operators.OperatorTrait.is_hermitian "
"\"qiskit_bosons.operators.OperatorTrait.is_hermitian\") compares an operator"
" with its adjoint in normal order, so it recognizes $b\\,b^\\dagger + "
"b^\\dagger b$ as Hermitian without further work.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"<Admonition title=\"Note\" type=\"note\">\n"
"  Normal ordering is an exact identity in the untruncated algebra, and `equiv` relies on that. It is *not* an identity after a truncation. A mapped operator depends on the form you map, so the mapped images of `contracted` and `reordered` above differ, on the highest retained level only. See [Truncate the Fock space](mappers#truncation-explanation) for the size of the difference and how to choose a form.\n"
"</Admonition>\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Order and filter terms {#order-and-filter-terms}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Reordering the *terms* of an operator, as opposed to the factors inside a "
"term, does not change the operator but can change the behavior of algorithms"
" that read the terms in order, such as a randomized product formula. The "
"functions of "
"[`qiskit_bosons.operators.terms`](https://docs.quantum.ibm.com/api/qiskit-"
"bosons/operators-terms#module-qiskit_bosons.operators.terms "
"\"qiskit_bosons.operators.terms\") return a new operator and keep any group "
"indices attached to their terms.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"[`canonical_order()`](https://docs.quantum.ibm.com/api/qiskit-"
"bosons/operators-terms-canonical-"
"order#qiskit_bosons.operators.terms.canonical_order "
"\"qiskit_bosons.operators.terms.canonical_order\") sorts the terms into a "
"fixed order that depends only on the written factors, so an operator reads "
"the same no matter how it was assembled. "
"[`order_terms()`](https://docs.quantum.ibm.com/api/qiskit-bosons/operators-"
"terms-order-terms#qiskit_bosons.operators.terms.order_terms "
"\"qiskit_bosons.operators.terms.order_terms\") sorts by a key that you "
"supply, which receives each term as the tuple that "
"[`iter_terms()`](https://docs.quantum.ibm.com/api/qiskit-bosons/operators-"
"operator-trait#qiskit_bosons.operators.OperatorTrait.iter_terms "
"\"qiskit_bosons.operators.OperatorTrait.iter_terms\") yields:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"If an operator carries group indices (see [Group operator terms: use the "
"operator structure](grouping#grouping-explanation)), the function "
"[`group_order()`](https://docs.quantum.ibm.com/api/qiskit-bosons/operators-"
"terms-group-order#qiskit_bosons.operators.terms.group_order "
"\"qiskit_bosons.operators.terms.group_order\") makes each group one "
"contiguous run of terms:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The last helper removes terms rather than reordering them. A term that is a "
"product of number operators, $b_i^\\dagger b_i$, only multiplies each Fock "
"state by a phase, so "
"[`filter_diagonal_terms()`](https://docs.quantum.ibm.com/api/qiskit-"
"bosons/operators-terms-filter-diagonal-"
"terms#qiskit_bosons.operators.terms.filter_diagonal_terms "
"\"qiskit_bosons.operators.terms.filter_diagonal_terms\") can drop the "
"constant, the number operators, and their products when that phase is "
"irrelevant for what you compute. It changes the operator *in place* and "
"requires a normal-ordered operator, which it does not check:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Only the two hopping terms remain. The on-site interaction and the number "
"operator are diagonal in the occupation basis, so they are removed together "
"with the constant. The repeated mode in the interaction is handled "
"correctly: $b_0^\\dagger b_0^\\dagger b_0 b_0$ is diagonal, while "
"$b_0^\\dagger b_0^\\dagger b_0$ would not be.\n"
msgstr ""

